From 24c458ab69a6d334b77e4ce28e395e429285d988 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <tristan75015@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 02:24:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Polycrystal: maximum number of iterations of the non linear
 solver

Option --non-linear-solver-maximum-iterations (20 by default, the value
which was a constant of the simulation): a resolution of the microscopic
equilibrium which does not converge within this number of iterations
fails, and the time step is divided by two. On the polycrystal of 6,400
grains, 8 to 15 resolutions per computation still converged when the
limit of 20 iterations stopped them.

The test UniaxialPolycrystalTest-MaximumIterations runs the time step of
the first test with NewtonK0, the tolerance of Eisenstat and Walker and one
iteration at most, a resolution taking four: it expects the division of
the time step, and no second iteration.
---
 polycrystal/CMakeLists.txt          | 18 ++++++++++++++++++
 polycrystal/README.md               |  3 ++-
 polycrystal/UniaxialPolycrystal.cxx | 28 +++++++++++++++++++++++++---
 3 files changed, 45 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/polycrystal/CMakeLists.txt b/polycrystal/CMakeLists.txt
index 5a7952c..89dbb19 100755
--- a/polycrystal/CMakeLists.txt
+++ b/polycrystal/CMakeLists.txt
@@ -111,4 +111,22 @@ mm_opera_hpc_add_checked_test(NAME UniaxialPolycrystalTestPar
   COMMAND mpirun -n 2 $<TARGET_FILE:uniaxial-polycrystal>
           ${_polycrystal_options}
           --macroscopic-stress-output-file polycrystal-test-par.res)
+# the maximum number of iterations of the non linear solver is taken into
+# account. With NewtonK0 and the tolerance of Eisenstat and Walker, whose
+# first linear solve is loose (the residual is about halved), a resolution
+# of this time step takes four iterations: with one iteration at most, the
+# first resolution fails and the time step is divided, which is expected
+# (in double, no resolution converges, and the temporal sequence fails
+# after its ten divisions; in float, the absolute tolerance on the residual
+# accepts the smaller time steps). A second iteration would reveal an
+# ignored option.
+add_test(NAME UniaxialPolycrystalTest-MaximumIterations
+  COMMAND $<TARGET_FILE:uniaxial-polycrystal> ${_polycrystal_options}
+          --macroscopic-stress-output-file polycrystal-test-iterations.res
+          --non-linear-solver NewtonK0
+          --adaptive-linear-solver-tolerance EisenstatWalker1
+          --non-linear-solver-maximum-iterations 1)
+set_tests_properties(UniaxialPolycrystalTest-MaximumIterations PROPERTIES
+  PASS_REGULAR_EXPRESSION "dividing time step by 2"
+  FAIL_REGULAR_EXPRESSION "Fixed point iteration  2")
 add_test(NAME PolycrystalTestWithPostProPar COMMAND mpirun -n 2 ./uniaxial-polycrystal --enable-export-von-Mises-stress --enable-export-first_eigen_stress --nstep 1 --duration 0.01)
diff --git a/polycrystal/README.md b/polycrystal/README.md
index e384f17..669f009 100644
--- a/polycrystal/README.md
+++ b/polycrystal/README.md
@@ -89,6 +89,7 @@ Usage: ./uniaxial-polycrystal --help
 | `--linear-solver <string>`                  | string | `HyprePCG`                   | Linear solver of the Newton method: a Krylov solver of hypre (`HyprePCG`, `HypreGMRES` or `HypreFGMRES`, preconditioned by `--linear-solver-preconditioner`), or a direct solver of mfem-mgis, `MUMPSSolver` or `CuDSSSolver` (on a CUDA GPU only), the matrices being given as general ones. Ignored by `NewtonK0`. |
 | `--reuse-analysis`, `--no-reuse-analysis`   | bool   | `false`                      | Reuse the analysis of the direct solver for the matrices of the same pattern (parameter `ReuseAnalysis` of mfem-mgis). Ignored, with a warning, without a direct solver, with `NewtonK0` or with PETSc. |
 | `--non-linear-solver <string>`              | string | `Newton`                     | Non linear solver: `Newton` or `NewtonK0` (tangent systems solved by a conjugate gradient preconditioned by the elastic operator).            |
+| `--non-linear-solver-maximum-iterations <int>` | int  | `20`                         | Maximum number of iterations of the non linear solver for each resolution of the microscopic equilibrium. A resolution which does not converge within this number of iterations fails, and the time step is divided by two (ten times at most per temporal sequence). |
 | `--adaptive-linear-solver-tolerance <string>` | string | `None`                       | Relative tolerance of the linear solver adapted to the decrease of the residual: `None`, `EisenstatWalker1` or `EisenstatWalker2`; requires an iterative linear solver. |
 | `--adaptive-linear-solver-initial-tolerance <double>` | double | `-1`                         | Initial adaptive tolerance, the default one if `-1`. Giving it without `--adaptive-linear-solver-tolerance` is an error.                      |
 | `--adaptive-linear-solver-maximum-tolerance <double>` | double | `-1` | Maximum adaptive tolerance, the default one (0.9) if `-1`: the tolerance of Eisenstat and Walker rises to it when the residual increases, for instance after a failure of the integration of the behaviour. Giving it without `--adaptive-linear-solver-tolerance` is an error. |
@@ -174,7 +175,7 @@ This table shows the comparison between the simulated Cauchy stress values and t
 
 ## Tests
 
-`ctest` runs `UniaxialPolycrystalTest` (one time step of 0.2 s without post-processing, whose axial stress `Szz` is compared to the one computed by Cast3M, `results/polycrystal_cast3m.csv`, with a relative tolerance of 1 %, by `cmake/run-and-check.py`, which requires Python 3; without it, the case is only run), `UniaxialPolycrystalTest-DisplacementGradient` (the same with `--displacement-gradient`), `UniaxialPolycrystalTestPar` (the same on 2 processes) and `PolycrystalTestWithPostProPar` (one time step of 0.01 s on 2 processes, exporting the von Mises stress and the first eigen stress). With the option `MM_OPERA_HPC_GPU_TESTS` (see the main README), `UniaxialPolycrystalTest-gpu` and `UniaxialPolycrystalTest-mixed-gpu` run the first test with `--execution gpu` and with `--execution mixed --behaviours-execution cpu`.
+`ctest` runs `UniaxialPolycrystalTest` (one time step of 0.2 s without post-processing, whose axial stress `Szz` is compared to the one computed by Cast3M, `results/polycrystal_cast3m.csv`, with a relative tolerance of 1 %, by `cmake/run-and-check.py`, which requires Python 3; without it, the case is only run), `UniaxialPolycrystalTest-DisplacementGradient` (the same with `--displacement-gradient`), `UniaxialPolycrystalTestPar` (the same on 2 processes), `UniaxialPolycrystalTest-MaximumIterations` (the first test with `--non-linear-solver NewtonK0 --adaptive-linear-solver-tolerance EisenstatWalker1 --non-linear-solver-maximum-iterations 1`: a resolution of this time step takes four iterations, so the first one fails; the test expects the division of the time step, and no second iteration) and `PolycrystalTestWithPostProPar` (one time step of 0.01 s on 2 processes, exporting the von Mises stress and the first eigen stress). With the option `MM_OPERA_HPC_GPU_TESTS` (see the main README), `UniaxialPolycrystalTest-gpu` and `UniaxialPolycrystalTest-mixed-gpu` run the first test with `--execution gpu` and with `--execution mixed --behaviours-execution cpu`.
 
 With the option `--single-material`, `UniaxialPolycrystalTest-SingleMaterial` compares the same axial stress to the one of Cast3M. `UniaxialPolycrystalTest-SingleMaterial-Comparison` and `UniaxialPolycrystalTestPar-SingleMaterial-Comparison` (on 2 processes) run two time steps of 0.2 s with and without the option, each run in its own directory, and compare the main output files and the averages of the stresses per grain (`avgStressPolycrystal`) of the two runs (`cmake/run-and-compare.py`): relative tolerances of 1e-9 on the main output file and of 2e-5 on the averages, written with 6 digits (1 % in single precision).
 
